Defconfigs this repository derives, because the fork does not ship them --
or ships them short of what a fleet board needs. An external tree's configs
take precedence over the fork's of the same name (buildroot's %_defconfig rules
are declared externals-first).

raspberrypi3_wpe_2_38_cog_defconfig is the fork's, plus what every fleet bench
system needs and the fork's cog images do not have (the rpi4 defconfig below
carries the same four):

  BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT*               the fork installs the Pi 3's WiFi
                                            firmware and no wpa_supplicant; the
                                            board has no cable at the bench
  BR2_PACKAGE_OPENSSH (not DROPBEAR)        dropbear 2019.78 predates ed25519
                                            keys and refuses the driving key
  BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES    ../board/linux-fleet.fragment: TUN
                                            and netfilter, without which
                                            tailscaled cannot create tailscale0
  BR2_PACKAGE_IPTABLES                      tailscaled's netfilter tool

The WPE buildroot fork release-pins its `cog` defconfigs for the **rpi3 only** --
raspberrypi3_wpe_{2_22,2_28,2_38,2_42,2_46,2_50}_cog_defconfig -- and its rpi4
defconfigs (raspberrypi4_wpe_defconfig and the _ml_/_weston_/_wst_ variants) pin
no WPE release at all. So "WPEWebKit 2.38 on an rpi4" is a configuration that
exists here and nowhere upstream, and the only honest way to have it is to derive
it from the two that do exist.

raspberrypi4_wpe_2_38_cog_defconfig is raspberrypi3_wpe_2_38_cog_defconfig with
the changes below. Each board change is taken from raspberrypi4_wpe_defconfig
rather than invented; the last two are what this repository needs of any image.

  BR2_cortex_a53 -> BR2_cortex_a72          the SoC
  DTS bcm2710-rpi-3-b... -> bcm2711-rpi-4-b the board
  post-build.sh, post-image.sh              board/raspberrypi3 -> board/raspberrypi4
  post-script args                          the rpi3 set carries --rpi-wifi and
                                            --overclock-pi3, which name the wrong
                                            board; the rpi4 set is used instead
  RPI_FIRMWARE_VARIANT_PI4                  the rpi4 boots start4.elf and
                                            fixup4.dat and no bootcode.bin, and
                                            board/raspberrypi4's genimage config
                                            names exactly those; the default
                                            variant installs the pre-4 set, so
                                            without this the build reaches
                                            genimage and dies on a missing
                                            rpi-firmware/fixup4.dat
  BR2_PACKAGE_WPA_SUPPLICANT*               wifi, see the defconfig
  BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2*                   the rpi3 2.38 defconfig emits a
                                            tarball only, which leaves genimage
                                            nothing to assemble into an
                                            sdcard.img

The cog platform is *not* among them. raspberrypi4_wpe_defconfig carries
BR2_PACKAGE_COG_PLATFORM_DRM=y, but that symbol depends on
BR2_PACKAGE_WPEBACKEND_FDO, which nothing selects and which itself needs an
EGL-on-wayland stack (mesa3d, wayland) no 2.38 configuration here builds -- so
kconfig drops the line, in the rpi4_wpe defconfig as much as in a copy of it.
Carrying a line kconfig discards would claim a backend the image does not have,
so it is not carried: this resolves, as the rpi3 does, to wpebackend-rdk's
bcm-rpi backend on dispmanx.

The kernel *tree* is not changed: the release-pinned rpi-5.15.y, which is what
keeps this a 2.38 configuration rather than a different distribution wearing
the release's name. The kernel *config* is: the fork's
board/raspberrypi/rpi34-linux-5.15.config is named for the rpi3 and the rpi4
but is a Pi 2/3 config in truth -- measured on an rpi4 (rev 1.5) over the
tryboot lane 2026-08-31, it hangs before init, and it lacks the 2711's SD
controller (MMC_SDHCI_IPROC), PCIe root (PCIE_BRCMSTB), XHCI, LPAE and
HIGHMEM, so it could mount no root there even if it ran. The rpi4 defconfig
uses the pinned tree's own in-tree bcm2711 defconfig instead (what RPi OS
ships as kernel7l), with the same fleet fragment on top.

TODO: **a full bench run on this image is unmeasured.** The tryboot lane and
the rescue's kernel are proven on the board; the first boot of this image's
own kernel, and the display half -- an rpi4's VideoCore VI has no /opt/vc GL
driver, and this resolves to the backend that wants one -- are not. A board
that boots to a shell and shows no page is the expected next question, not a
surprise.
